Fabric read scope and result contracts
======================================

Network client reads and statistics always intersect organization membership
with both optional filters: a single requested site and a set of granted sites.
Neither filter replaces tenant authorization; an explicitly empty set yields no
clients. Deleted sites remain excluded through the shared organization predicate.
The Fabric network handler supplies its accessible_site_ids to that service.

The Fabric firewall-alert handler supplies the same invocation scope to
FirewallService's existing organization/site device filter. It unpacks the
service's (rows, total) return value and serializes the actual alert rows,
including signature_name. Its output count is the number of returned rows, not
the total across all pages. An empty page has count zero and an empty alerts list.

Fabric storage health filters controllers by organization, nondeleted site and
any explicit invocation site scope before a storage provider is contacted.
An inaccessible explicit controller returns no appliance rows. Provider behavior
and vendor health interpretation are unchanged.

These handlers return fixed database read failures without raw exception details.
They preserve the existing None/unrestricted scope convention and do not change
hybrid author grant policy. Author verification, generic permission ceilings,
other module read paths and device write qualification remain separate controls.
